A||N||21||Seanh||LT or LS engine? Whats the difference?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||05-05-2001||02:20 PM||||I am looking into a 350 swap on my new 87 GT. If I do it, I want to go with the best obviously. I don't want to spend all that money for a kit and then swap in a standard 350 engine. What s the difference between all these V8? I was told by 3 different people to go with either LS1, LT1 or LT4. Whats the differences? Which would put out the most power? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||05-05-2001||05:26 PM||||Well, since nobody I know makes an LS1 swap kit yet. The LT1 and LT4 are basically the same motor with cylinder head and camshaft revisions that give the Lt4 more power. If you wanted to, you could buy an LT1 then get the parts and build the LT4 yourself. So your best bet would be to get an LT1 and get some aftermarket parts for it, unless you just want to throw a motor in it and be done with it. Then the LT4 would be already assembled and have more power, stock for stock. Or you could just wait for Archie to finish his LS1 kit and pay lots of money to have an all aluminum V8 that makes about the same hp. That's if you don't want to just build a high HP pre LT1 v8 and have more hp (usually the less costly route).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||05-05-2001||05:33 PM||||Well, I was just wondering the differences. I haven't ruled out a regular 350 yet either. My only "must have" is fuel injection. You would reccomend just going the route of a regular 350 and building it up? I have all the time in world. I would just buy it and work on it in my grage until Im ready to buy Archie's kit and install it. Is a LT1 going to cost much more then building up a 350? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||05-05-2001||08:51 PM||||Your best bet would be to talk to Archie and see what he thinks. Also check the junkyards and maybe some GM performance parts dealers and see what kind of deals you can get.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.spoperformanceparts.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spoperformanceparts.com/</A> <BR>as good a place to start as any<p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 05-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||frontal lobe||05-05-2001||11:30 PM||||I got an LT1 out of a '96 Buick Roadmaster.  Had 30,000 miles and got totaled in a t-bone type accident, so no front end damage.  Has the fuel injection on it and the wiring harness and ecm intact.  I was going to try the conversion, but after getting it, found out that for LT1's, you have to move the engine over a couple of inches which means you have to notch the passenger rear frame, and also have to get new axles because shifting it over a couple inches makes the axles not fit.  Archie sells the axles.<P>Anyway, that makes it too big of a project for me so I'm selling it if you are interested.  I'm in suburban Milwaukee.<P>LT1's in that model had the iron heads and so about 265 hp and 330 torque.  Same engine used in the impala SS's of that generation.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ray b||05-06-2001||12:55 AM||||LS1 all alloy but no hotrod parts and old stuff don't fit.<BR>BRODEX makes old style alloy block that all hotrod parts fit at $2500 each or hunt up a old chevy alloy block they are hard to find but out there somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sjp777||05-06-2001||01:08 AM||||LT1 is the engine from the 93 to 97 vettes, T/A's and the good Camaro's. It was replaced by the LS1 in 98. LT1's put out about 275hp, I think the LS1 is right around 330. At that kind of hp in a Fiero, who cares? Either one will smoke just about any car on the road.<BR>The big draw for the LSI is that it's aluminum with a composite intake. I don't have hard numbers, but I would imagine it's right around the same weight as the stock 2.8. No weight gain and 330hp? Oh my.<P>You can get an LT1 used complete with injection setup, wiring harness and 'puter for about $2000 to $2500 depending on mileage. Plan on about $1500 to $2000 more for the LSI (around $6000 new) and you'll still be waiting for Archie to come up with the install kit.<P>Just a thought: L98's are plentiful, cheap, and you can get brand new aluminum vette heads for them for about $500/pair. Figure about 240 to 250hp with that setup. Still plenty for a Fiero.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||05-06-2001||01:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sjp777:<BR><B>LT1 is the engine from the 93 to 97 vettes, T/A's and the good Camaro's. It was replaced by the LS1 in 98. LT1's put out about 275hp, I think the LS1 is right around 330. At that kind of hp in a Fiero, who cares? Either one will smoke just about any car on the road.<BR>The big draw for the LSI is that it's aluminum with a composite intake. I don't have hard numbers, but I would imagine it's right around the same weight as the stock 2.8. No weight gain and 330hp? Oh my.<P>You can get an LT1 used complete with injection setup, wiring harness and 'puter for about $2000 to $2500 depending on mileage. Plan on about $1500 to $2000 more for the LSI (around $6000 new) and you'll still be waiting for Archie to come up with the install kit.<P>Just a thought: L98's are plentiful, cheap, and you can get brand new aluminum vette heads for them for about $500/pair. Figure about 240 to 250hp with that setup. Still plenty for a Fiero.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And the L98 is from what?.....<P>||||reg||5
Z||000008||Seanh||05-06-2001||01:48 PM||||I see an entire LT4 upper end on ebay for like $1000 right now. I also see a 96 LT1 engine for $700 with no upper end except for heads and valve covers. Good price? If someone doesn't run up the price.||||reg||1
Z||000009||sjp777||05-06-2001||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> And the L98 is from what?.....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>L98 is from the pre-93 IROC's, T/A's and Formula 350's. Basically the 5.7 TPI motor. The vettes had the same motor, but with aluminum heads<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||sjp777||05-06-2001||05:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>I see an entire LT4 upper end on ebay for like $1000 right now. I also see a 96 LT1 engine for $700 with no upper end except for heads and valve covers. Good price? If someone doesn't run up the price.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Killer price if it's not trashed. You can't use the LT4 intake with the LT1 heads. the ports are different.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||05-06-2001||05:47 PM||||That LT4 intake setup includes the LT4 heads. And the engine includes LT1 heads. Proablly go with the LT4's.<P>I know that price is damn good for the engine, but $1K just for the upper end sounds a little steep, but it does include heads, fuel injectors, all the electronics and harness, pretty much everthing. Is that price too much for the intake setup?<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 05-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||sjp777||05-06-2001||06:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>That LT4 intake setup includes the LT4 heads. And the engine includes LT1 heads. Proablly go with the LT4's.<P>I know that price is damn good for the engine, but $1K just for the upper end sounds a little steep, but it does include heads, fuel injectors, all the electronics and harness, pretty much everthing. Is that price too much for the intake setup?<P>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 05-06-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you kidding? the harness alone goes for about $300. LT4 heads are rare and therefore spendy, as is the intake and larger injectors. Check out the TPIS website. They want $2500 for a refurbished TPI setup from the L98, and that price does not include the motor!<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||05-06-2001||06:09 PM||||Ooch. I might just have to get that setup then. I hate to have to drive all that way just to get it, but it sounds like a heck of a deal then. I have an 82 Crossfire injection system, manifold and harness from a 305 Z-28, maybe I should sell it!||||reg||1
Z||000014||sjp777||05-06-2001||06:39 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>Ooch. I might just have to get that setup then. I hate to have to drive all that way just to get it, but it sounds like a heck of a deal then. I have an 82 Crossfire injection system, manifold and harness from a 305 Z-28, maybe I should sell it!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Or throw it away. Cross fire injection was GM's failed experiment with modern fuel injection and was thankfully quickly abandonned. Yuk<P>And I checked out that LT4 set up on ebay, it's up to $1025 but still a great deal. It looks very complete. All you would need is the LT1 short block and you would have a Fiero with a Firehawk engine. Pretty unbeatable<p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||05-06-2001||06:52 PM||||I have wondered why they quite using it. Why did it fail, I mean what kinda problems did they have with it? This was in my friends 82 Z-28 and it easily beat most cars in town, he's just putting a carbed 350 in there so I got his old engine. I always thought Crossfire was pretty awesome, and we never had any trouble with his car, well, fuel system related anyway. I guess busted cam shafts wouldn't have anything to do with crossfire. I'v also given thought to building just a 350 and putting his 305 heads on it and crossfire manifold. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||rockcrawl||05-06-2001||09:49 PM||||I don't think there's anything wrong with crossfire injection, it's just not as good as a port injection system. The crossfire works fine, it's just not a big power system.||||reg||1
Z||000017||sjp777||05-08-2001||11:03 PM||||And for those really looking to be the king of the hill and with too much money to burn, There is such a thing as the LT5... If you can find one, and are willing to take a second mortgage on your house to get it.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Pontiaddict||05-09-2001||06:02 PM||||Why go with an LT5 when an LS1 is cheaper and can make just as much hp?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Seanh||05-09-2001||07:33 PM||||Yeah, Iv heard of the LT5's, even seen one at a car show/swap meet a couple weeks ago. I don't think I talked to the owner, but I recall wondering what was so special about that engine and why it was so costly. What's the deal with LT5's ? Yet another head/camshaft modification?||||reg||1
Z||000020||ray b||05-09-2001||07:55 PM||||LT5 is that the 4cam vett motor?<BR>rare and very very priceee peice<BR>chevy also recycles names as 1970 LT-1<BR>was a 350hp 350 on the old system then.||||reg||1
Z||000021||saleenfiero||05-09-2001||08:04 PM||||LS-1's are 330 HP now (camaro), or 350 (vette), or do a little head work, BOOM, it turns into an LS-6 as seen in the vette Z06 with 385, soon to be 405 next year.<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
